"""Explicitly authored aeolian-process material; no generated teaching prose."""
from assemble_topic import assemble
FACTS={
'01':['Wind abrasion wears a target surface with moving sediment; deflation removes loose material; attrition wears moving particles through collisions.','Wind processes depend on available sediment, surface cohesion, moisture, vegetation and wind conditions, not heat alone.'],
'01-01':['Wind abrasion involves particle contact scraping or striking an exposed target surface.','Evidence of a strong wind alone does not establish abrasive wear without a target and suitable sediment contact.'],
'01-02':['Deflation is removal of loose particles by wind.','Loss of fines can lower a surface or leave a relatively coarser cover without adding gravel.'],
'01-03':['Wind attrition wears transported particles through collisions with each other.','Particle-to-particle wear differs from abrasion of a fixed surface, even where both occur during transport.'],
'02':['Rock pedestals, deflation hollows and desert pavements are distinct residual or erosional features.','The observed form needs a supported mechanism; water availability at an oasis does not explain wind excavation.'],
'02-01':['Differential abrasion near the ground can help leave a narrower pedestal stem beneath a broader top.','Rock resistance and other weathering processes can also contribute, so shape alone does not prove a unique origin.'],
'02-02':['A deflation hollow develops through removal of loose material that lowers the ground.','An oasis concerns accessible water and is not interchangeable with the excavation history of a hollow.'],
'02-03':['Selective removal of fines can concentrate coarse fragments as a desert pavement in the stated model.','Some pavement histories involve additional processes, including dust accumulation and soil development.'],
'03':['Wind transport includes suspension within air, saltation in repeated hops and surface creep along the ground.','Particle movement mode depends on conditions and can change; multiple modes can occur together.'],
'03-01':['Fine dust can remain suspended in turbulent air for a substantial interval and travel far from its source.','A cloud’s appearance alone does not measure concentration or identify every particle source.'],
'03-02':['Saltation is repeated short hopping with intermittent surface contact.','An impacting saltating grain can help initiate movement of other grains.'],
'03-03':['Surface creep involves rolling or sliding grains along the ground, sometimes assisted by saltation impacts.','Aeolian surface creep is a particle-transport mode, distinct from downslope soil creep as a mass movement.'],
'04':['Dunes are depositional sand accumulations shaped by wind regime, sediment supply and surface conditions.','Star dunes have multiple arms around a central high area; linear dunes have elongated ridges.'],
'04-01':['Star dunes occur in the Sossusvlei area and have multi-armed plan forms consistent with winds from different directions over time.','Height, colour or a regional name alone does not establish that a particular dune is a star.'],
'04-02':['Linear or seif dunes are elongated ridges, commonly influenced by more than one dominant wind direction in suitable regimes.','One instantaneous wind observation does not establish the long-term wind regime that built a large dune.']}
CARDS={
'001':('Describe the defining actions of abrasion, deflation and attrition by wind.','Abrasion uses moving grains to wear a target surface. Deflation removes loose material from the ground. Attrition wears transported grains as they collide with one another.',['1 mark for the abrasion action step; 1 for removal in deflation; 1 for load-particle collisions and their wear result.']),
'002':('Describe the processes supported by these observations: A = sand wears a rock face; B = fines leave a patch and gravel remains; C = moving grains lose corners through collisions with each other.','A supports abrasion of a surface. B supports deflation and residual coarse concentration. C supports attrition of the load.',['1 mark for the A contact/target relationship; 1 for interpreting B’s removal evidence; 1 for C’s particle-collision feature.']),
'003':('Describe the abrasion evidence: airborne or hopping sand repeatedly strikes a low rock face, where measured wear develops; no comparable wear is detected on a sheltered face.','The worn face receives sediment impacts. The sheltered-face comparison supports a contact-related difference. The observations are consistent with abrasion, not wind force alone proving wear.',['1 mark for the impact–wear relationship; 1 for the exposed/sheltered observation contrast; 1 for a supported abrasion interpretation.']),
'004':('Describe the deflation pattern: an exposed patch loses fine loose material downwind, its surface lowers, and the visible gravel proportion increases without a recorded addition of stones.','Fine material is removed and the patch lowers. Gravel becomes a larger share of the exposed surface because fines are lost. The evidence supports deflation, without requiring added gravel.',['1 mark for the removal/lowering pattern; 1 for the gravel-proportion evidence; 1 for the residual-concentration interpretation.']),
'005':('Describe the attrition evidence: marked transported grains repeatedly collide with other grains, lose projecting corners and become rounder during the observed sequence.','The collisions occur between moving particles. Corner loss changes the grains themselves. The rounder result supports attrition rather than only abrasion of a fixed rock face.',['1 mark for the particle-to-particle relationship; 1 for the corner-loss evidence; 1 for interpreting rounding as load wear.']),
'006':('Describe the selective-removal sequence that can create a desert pavement in a deflation model.','The surface initially contains fines and coarser fragments. Wind removes exposed fine material more readily under the stated conditions. Coarse fragments remain as a more continuous cover that can shelter underlying material.',['1 mark for the first mixed-surface stage; 1 for selective fine removal; 1 for residual coarse cover as the result.']),
'007':('Describe and distinguish the wind-erosion features in the supplied supplied resource: the residual rock, lowered loose-sediment patch and coarse surface cover.','A narrow rock stem beneath a wider top is consistent with a pedestal. The lowered patch supports a deflation hollow. A concentrated gravel cover supports desert pavement in the specified selective-removal sequence.',['1 mark for the pedestal form feature; 1 for the hollow’s lowering evidence; 1 for the pavement-cover relationship.']),
'008':('Describe the pedestal interpretation: a rock has a narrower lower stem and wider top; repeated sand impacts and greater wear are recorded near the lower zone; its upper rock is more resistant.','The rock has a pedestal-like profile. Near-ground sand impacts support stronger lower abrasion, while the resistant upper material also helps the top survive. The evidence supports combined controls rather than shape alone proving one cause.',['1 mark for the narrow-stem/wide-top feature; 1 for the impact/wear evidence; 1 for the role of resistance in the interpretation.']),
'009':('Describe the hollow evidence: fixed survey points show the centre of a loose-sediment patch lowering by 15 cm over the observation period while wind removes fines and the rocky edge changes little.','Lowering is concentrated in the patch centre. Removal of fines provides a wind-excavation mechanism. The contrast with the rocky edge supports a deflation hollow rather than an accumulating dune.',['1 mark for the central-lowering pattern with value; 1 for the fine-removal evidence; 1 for the hollow-versus-accumulation interpretation.']),
'010':('Describe desert-pavement evidence: gravel cover rises from 40% to 70% while fines are removed; no outside gravel addition is recorded.','The exposed surface becomes more gravel-dominated. The 30-percentage-point increase accompanies fine-material loss. This supports residual concentration rather than proving extra stones were deposited.',['1 mark for the gravel-dominated pattern; 1 for the 40%–70% evidence correctly interpreted; 1 for distinguishing removal-driven concentration from addition.']),
'011':('Describe suspension, saltation and surface creep as wind-transport movements.','Suspension carries particles within the air. Saltation moves grains in repeated short hops with landings. Surface creep rolls or slides grains along the ground.',['1 mark for the airborne movement step; 1 for hopping and contacts; 1 for rolling/sliding with the different surface-contact result.']),
'012':('Describe the transport modes in the supplied original path diagram, using the airborne, hopping and ground-contact paths.','The sustained airborne path supports suspension. Repeated short hops support saltation. Rolling or sliding along the surface supports creep; the movement evidence is more diagnostic than drawn size alone.',['1 mark for the airborne-path feature; 1 for the hop/contact evidence; 1 for the ground-contact movement relationship.']),
'013':('Describe the suspension evidence: fine particles are recorded above ground throughout an observation interval without visible surface contact, while larger grains remain at rest below.','The fine material is being carried within the air. The contrast with resting coarse grains shows selective movement under those conditions. The record supports suspension of the fine particles, not all sediment at the site.',['1 mark for the airborne feature; 1 for the fine/coarse observation contrast; 1 for a correctly limited suspension interpretation.']),
'014':('Describe the saltation pattern: a time sequence places a grain on the surface, then above it, then landing again, with the sequence repeated.','The grain makes repeated short hops. Each hop is separated by a landing. The alternating airborne and contact observations support saltation rather than prolonged suspension.',['1 mark for the repeated-hop pattern; 1 for the landing evidence; 1 for the suspension contrast.']),
'015':('Describe the surface-creep evidence: a coarse grain begins rolling after a hopping grain strikes it, remains in ground contact and later stops.','The coarse grain moves along the ground. The impact provides a plausible trigger for rolling. Its maintained contact supports surface creep during that interval, not sustained airborne transport.',['1 mark for the ground-contact pattern; 1 for the impact/rolling observation; 1 for the surface-creep interpretation.']),
'016':('Describe a sequence by which wind can build a sand dune.','Wind transports available sand. A local reduction in transporting conditions or an obstacle allows some grains to accumulate. The deposit alters airflow and further deposition and redistribution can build a mound or ridge.',['1 mark for the first sand-transport step; 1 for trapping or local deposition; 1 for accumulation/redistribution as the dune-building result.']),
'017':('Describe the dune-form contrasts in the supplied original plan resource, using its ridge arrangement and scale.','One dune has arms radiating around a central high area, supporting a star form. The other has a long narrow crest, supporting a linear form. Shape and scaled proportions provide the evidence, rather than colour or a place name alone.',['1 mark for the radial-arm feature; 1 for the elongated-crest contrast; 1 for using scale/proportion evidence in classification.']),
'018':('Describe the star-dune interpretation: an original Sossusvlei teaching sketch has four ridges meeting around a central peak; the long-term wind record includes several directions.','The ridges form a multi-armed plan around a central high area. That shape supports a star dune. Multiple long-term wind directions are consistent with deposition and redistribution on different sides.',['1 mark for the radial-ridge pattern; 1 for the central-peak/arm evidence; 1 for linking form to the supplied multi-directional regime.']),
'019':('Describe the linear-dune evidence: an original plan has a crest 800 m long and 40 m wide, with neighbouring elongated ridges; only today’s wind direction is measured.','The ridge is strongly elongated, with a 20:1 length-to-width ratio. Neighbouring parallel elongated forms support a linear-dune interpretation. Today’s wind alone cannot establish the long-term formation regime.',['1 mark for the elongated pattern; 1 for the ratio/adjacent-ridge evidence; 1 for the justified limit on wind-history inference.']),
'020':('Explain the error in calling grain-to-grain corner loss “abrasion of a rock face”. What contact would distinguish the processes?','The confusion is between attrition of the load and abrasion of a target surface. Grain-to-grain wear supports attrition; grains scraping a fixed face provide the test for abrasion of that face.',['1 mark for the attrition/abrasion confusion; 1 for the correct contact distinction and discriminating test.']),
'021':('Explain why describing a spring and nearby trees does not explain formation of a deflation hollow. What evidence would test the excavation process?','The error confuses water availability at an oasis with wind removal creating a depression. Evidence of fines removed and surface lowering would test deflation; the spring alone does not establish excavation.',['1 mark for the oasis/hollow confusion; 1 for the correct wind-removal/lowering test.']),
'022':('Explain the error in classifying a multi-armed dune as linear just because it is in a desert. The plan shows four ridges meeting at a central peak.','The error substitutes a regional label for morphology. Multiple radiating arms support a star form; an elongated crest is the relevant contrasting test for a linear dune.',['1 mark for the place-versus-form confusion; 1 for the correct radial-versus-elongated distinction applied as a test to the observed plan.'])}
TASKS=[
{'objectives':['01','02','03'],'prompt':'(a) Define deflation. [1]\n(b) Describe the movement of suspension, saltation and surface creep. [3]\n(c) Name the landform with a narrow lower rock stem beneath a broader top. [1]\n(d) Describe two observations supporting a removal-driven gravel pavement: fines are recorded leaving a mixed surface while the same coarse fragments form a more continuous cover. [2]','answer':'(a) Removal of loose particles by wind. (b) Suspension is airborne carriage; saltation repeated hopping; creep rolling/sliding along the ground. (c) Rock pedestal. (d) Fines are lost rather than added; coarse cover becomes more continuous without requiring an increase in the coarse fragments.','guide':['(a) 1 A mark for the definition point of loose-material removal by wind.','(b) 3 A marks for the distinct movement descriptions, one per mode.','(c) 1 A mark for the landform name rock pedestal.','(d) 2 B marks for interpreting the recorded fine loss and residual-coarse-cover evidence.'],'split':{'A':5,'B':2},'parts':[('a','Define',1,'A'),('b','Describe',3,'A'),('c','Name',1,'A'),('d','Describe',2,'B')]},
{'objectives':['02','03','04'],'prompt':'A supplied resource records A as four ridges radiating from a central peak, B as a crest 800 m long and 40 m wide, and C as a bare patch whose fines leave downwind while the ground lowers 15 cm.\n(a) Name the process removing fines at C. [1]\n(b) Describe the form and classification contrast between A and B. [3]\n(c) Describe two observations at C that support a hollow-forming interpretation. [2]\n(d) What judgement is supported about whether one present-day wind arrow proves the long-term regime that made A? [1]','answer':'(a) Deflation. (b) A has radiating arms around a centre, consistent with star; B is elongated with a 20:1 ratio, consistent with linear. (c) Fine loss and measured surface lowering support excavation. (d) No: one current direction does not establish the long-term multi-directional regime.','guide':['(a) 1 A mark for deflation.','(b) 3 B marks for A’s radial form, B’s elongated proportional evidence and the correctly contrasted classifications.','(c) 2 B marks for the fine-removal and lowering data, both interpreted as evidence of excavation.','(d) 1 C mark for a supported decision rejecting one current arrow as proof of the long-term regime.'],'split':{'A':1,'B':5,'C':1},'parts':[('a','Name',1,'A'),('b','Describe',3,'B'),('c','Describe',2,'B'),('d','What',1,'C')]},
{'objectives':['01-02','02-03','03'],'prompt':'How far do you agree that increasing gravel cover proves new gravel was added? At a hypothetical site, cover increases from 40% to 70%; collectors record fines leaving downwind; repeated images identify the same coarse fragments and no gravel input is recorded. Judge the explanation of the observed surface change, recognising the limits of the observation. [5]','answer':'Disagree with proof of addition. The surface can become more gravel-dominated when fines are removed, so higher cover need not require added stones. Fine loss and the repeated identification of coarse fragments support residual concentration through deflation. The observations do not rule out every unrecorded input, but addition is not established; removal is the supported explanation for the stated interval.','guide':['Up to 4 C marks for judging the cover-percentage evidence, fine-removal mechanism, repeated-fragment record and limitation concerning unrecorded inputs. Reward causal reasoning rather than unrelated lists of wind processes.','1 C mark for a supported decision that increased cover alone does not prove addition.'],'split':{'C':5},'parts':[('a','How far do you agree',5,'C')]}
]
SOURCES=[('NPS-AEOLIAN','https://www.nps.gov/articles/aeolian.htm'),('NPS-LANDFORMS','https://home.nps.gov/subjects/geology/aeolian-landforms.htm'),('NASA-NAMIB','https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/namib-sand-sea-149130/'),('NASA-LINEAR','https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/sandscapes-of-the-namib-desert-146138/'),('UNESCO-NAMIB','https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1430/')]
